One of the first commercially successful reflex sights, the Nydar Model 47, was made by the Swain Nelson company and introduced in 1945, just a little bit right after the end of the Second World War. It was initially intended as a sight for shotgun hunters to aid in firing at flying birds, but it has also found limited use in the early days of old school tactical weapon modifications.
^orange;+10% Weapon Proficiency.^reset;

^green;Right-click on a compatible GiC Weapon to attach.^reset;

The PK-120 (GRAU index 1P87) is a sight that was part of the Russian Ratnik program in order to bring the Russian Armed Forces into the modern age. The PK-120 is intended to be the Russian answer to the American made EOTech sight after observing a noticeable improvement to overall combat effectiveness in units equipped with such a optic when compared those that are not equipped with any optics.
^orange;+10% Weapon Proficiency.^reset; 
^green;Right-click on a compatible GiC Weapon to attach.^reset;

The PK-AA is a more advanced version of the PK-A reflex sight manufactured by BelOMO (Belarusian Optical & Mechanical Association). The PK-AA sight was developed specifically for the needs of Venezuela to withstand the country's humid climate, high temperature, and tropical weather conditions.

^orange;+10% Weapon Proficiency.^reset;

^green;Right-click on a compatible GiC Weapon to attach.^reset;

The PK-AS is an advanced reflex sight manufactured by BelOMO (Belarusian Optical & Mechanical Association). It is a unique advanced scope as it is powered, but if the battery dies, the red dot becomes a black dot, allowing the wielder to still know where they are aiming if their sight loses power. It is used quite frequently by the Russian army. 
